#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "88fe37cf-5df2-5988-90b8-082f7aaca609" :question/math-academy-id "e-6791" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + y = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 2, \\quad y'(\\pi) = -1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A \\cos x + B \\sin x\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find constants $A$ and $B.$ To do this, we apply the initial conditions $y(\\pi)=2,$ $y'(\\pi)=-1.$\n\n- Substituting $y(\\pi) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =2 \\\\ A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  & =2 \\\\ -A & =2 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $y'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\ny' =  - A \\sin{x} + B\\cos{x},\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(\\pi)=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ -A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  & =-1 \\\\ -B & =-1 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -2\\cos{x} + \\sin{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "4739ced1-bbcc-516b-971f-a580ec97961a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6861" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y - 2y =0,\n\\ \\ y(1) = e, \\ \\ y'(1) = e ^{-2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=e$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =A{e}^{1}+B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ e & =Ae+B{e}^{-2} \\\\ A & =1-B{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = e^{-2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = Ae^x - 2Be^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1)=e^{-2}$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =A{e}^{1}-2B{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & =Ae-2B{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A=1-B{e}^{-3} \\\\ {e}^{-2}=Ae-2B{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = 1 - B e ^{-3}$ in the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2} & =(1-B{e}^{-3})e-2B{e}^{-2} \\\\ 1 & ={e}^{3}-B-2B \\\\ B & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $B= \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3$ back into the equation $A = 1-Be^{-3}$ gives\n\n$$\nA = 1 -  \\dfrac {(e ^ 3 - 1) e ^ {-3}} 3 =   \\dfrac {2 +  e ^ {-3}} 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny =  \\dfrac {  e ^ {-3} + 2} 3  e^{x} + \\dfrac {e ^ 3 - 1} 3 e^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "95cdd4cc-df70-5dac-8bd3-66edb012cdcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6862" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 4 y' + 4y =0,\n\\qquad y(0) =  3, \\qquad y'(0) = 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda = 2,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=3$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 3 & =A{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y' (0) = 2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny'  = 2 A e ^ {2 x} +  B e ^ { 2 x} + 2 B x e ^{ 2 x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+0 \\\\ 2 & =2A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3=A \\\\ 2=2A+B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=3$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =6+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3$ and $B=-4,$ and therefore the solution is \n\n$$\ny = 3 e ^ { 2 x } - 4 x e ^ { 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "80968044-25e2-5906-929c-b944f84ca27d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7051" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 6y' + 25y=0, \\quad y(0)= 2,  \\quad y'(0) = 5, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( P \\cos 4x + Q \\sin 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = 2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2 = P.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 5,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+{e}^{3x}(-4P\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(0) = 5$ and $P=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{e}^{0}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+{e}^{0}(-4(2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+4Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 5 & =6+4Q \\\\ -1 & =4Q \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{3x} \\left( 2 \\cos 4x - \\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin 4x \\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419e161a-490d-5c6f-a7e4-eaa1b01c0887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431471} {:answer/id #uuid "596a6ddd-3d0f-5678-9be7-860b0f40740a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431478} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1aafac-6504-5529-8eb6-e512b18e650b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431491} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfe6c00-24b3-5751-94bf-30b104f0e51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431512} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9a2e7c-4ae2-58e1-bf2d-a13b03f1e88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef9a2e7c-4ae2-58e1-bf2d-a13b03f1e88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431515} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f2e16b-2b82-5cc9-a8ad-cf8d984e234d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbabadfa-0d4d-5a45-80b7-bf364847e945" :question/math-academy-id "q-130339" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+3B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+3B=0.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ -3A+3B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d40d78b-f10f-551a-bf45-f2b3a9619d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}-{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431460} {:answer/id #uuid "1029e982-1cb8-59a1-800a-6eddc8bb0c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431462} {:answer/id #uuid "204931ce-cb59-5974-b135-26060d7cd2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-x}-3{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431466} {:answer/id #uuid "4e18fb50-aa99-53db-bb7e-d22c8a9849c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-x}+2{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431475} {:answer/id #uuid "8d32c42c-4dc2-5fb7-b932-58cc4bb05b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{8x}-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1029e982-1cb8-59a1-800a-6eddc8bb0c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431462} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b57be19-b31a-5529-8b7d-a45f836917f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d99b4ca2-1051-5b19-b0ca-e022766a3c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-130340" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-7{y}^{′}-8y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{8x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-7{y}^{′}-8y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=-18.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=0.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-18,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+8B{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-18$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-A+8B=-18.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ 8B-A=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}-2{e}^{8x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e031bec-4024-5bff-a0a7-6c68243bedda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431484} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b5524f-6144-5f88-9a69-8e05643972e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431493} {:answer/id #uuid "b8613441-e69b-5939-ae74-8cc30a7bd06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431499} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6783de-fb3d-5d54-8286-c1aa1c50555e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431514} {:answer/id #uuid "f1cb52c6-ae5e-5d19-9335-21304dab5d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-x}+2{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed6783de-fb3d-5d54-8286-c1aa1c50555e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431514} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd7eb07-6ad6-5159-a544-24234ed2ccad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93cffb5e-8615-53c1-a574-533e068512ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-130343" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+2B{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+2B=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -A+2B=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2,B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0843ecfa-4e3a-592f-95c8-ebdbd00c572f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-3x}-x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431457} {:answer/id #uuid "0922b0c1-b8d5-5acc-824f-3d66df13cd2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431459} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbb7a89-524b-5fb7-8987-365ee58f1aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{3x}+2x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431469} {:answer/id #uuid "af81ca3e-eea1-5fa8-b729-88ef645ad2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431498} {:answer/id #uuid "f7aec91b-5023-511f-94e5-52c5a358d29c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0922b0c1-b8d5-5acc-824f-3d66df13cd2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431459} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82527bc-a697-52b6-866e-a3ae6fadb46e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da0e1bd5-5ab3-52c5-b411-da88433bbf6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130346" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda =-3,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=-2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+B=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=-2$ gives $B=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090f99d5-9625-5409-8bfa-8e2c4cd59aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}+8x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431458} {:answer/id #uuid "203c8f4e-c8c1-5cb3-9c03-69fcae139338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}-2x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431465} {:answer/id #uuid "419328d7-32fd-5588-ac38-f02df1730edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}-x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431470} {:answer/id #uuid "da315c42-36d1-50ab-b060-34532474faac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}-7x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431511} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f42718-e600-5ca9-99e4-263408373f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}+x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f42718-e600-5ca9-99e4-263408373f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-7x}+x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431518} :answer-field/id #uuid "b628b5a8-23fe-5a39-8e61-e3f1bcd259c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a808d33-3853-58bc-a004-7d852404000e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130347" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+14{y}^{′}+49y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-7x}+Bx{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+14{y}^{′}+49y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=-1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=8,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7A{e}^{-7x}+B{e}^{-7x}-7Bx{e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-7A+B=8.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=-1$ gives $B=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-7x}+x{e}^{-7x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc56a83-a0f5-5d43-aa8f-5d18e267ba12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431488} {:answer/id #uuid "924b9c7c-fba8-53a4-8c7d-12b59a208e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431489} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7514f0-79da-5951-b071-38c575f33ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431497} {:answer/id #uuid "c65bb0d4-b401-58a8-9321-68e4246fafaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431505} {:answer/id #uuid "d575387f-ab5a-5abb-b0cd-aa50f48971a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d575387f-ab5a-5abb-b0cd-aa50f48971a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431510} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e73778b-1989-56f5-a344-4df7b08156a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00d6379f-1cac-59b7-a19a-344de30af15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130351" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x)-B\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=A\\sqrt{7}\\,⟹\\,A=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{7}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4c76a5-9a78-55cd-9899-c617f65dbb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431463} {:answer/id #uuid "454410df-f8e1-54f6-9e27-a2798142e99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡11x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431473} {:answer/id #uuid "4e27ee5a-1f56-520e-bd93-b277256b9d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431476} {:answer/id #uuid "5b280312-42b5-5848-bb8a-62dfe17d7a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431480} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ad627a-9899-5f34-94c4-32a5c8fa8625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e27ee5a-1f56-520e-bd93-b277256b9d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431476} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac988a1-6644-54b0-ad8b-5e3dc0ec73cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8669bf2-0ec8-592b-af07-c83c24aa14e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130354" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+121y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+121y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=11A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)-11B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0=11A\\,⟹\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfe40da-51cf-5cfc-8bf3-d8d61eb492c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263115} {:answer/id #uuid "41137a18-3b7b-5a66-99b6-7227a71e6489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263578} {:answer/id #uuid "45cd59f9-c985-59bb-b396-6d5cf9a8a8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263613} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a7c280-30ea-5792-a4e6-5f3be8233ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264434} {:answer/id #uuid "e54d24eb-37ad-5a49-959c-d7dc616c5073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bfe40da-51cf-5cfc-8bf3-d8d61eb492c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263115} :answer-field/id #uuid "5179e9c4-31c5-5342-81c7-5cb9365f2374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1d3d599-7dfd-5eae-928a-1ef2cf2f900f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130356" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+144y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-144 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 12\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q=1\\,⟹\\,Q=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-12P\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x+12Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=24$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12P=24\\,⟹\\,P=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡12x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡12x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db04b57-7e0b-51b5-9068-627b530d93d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431461} {:answer/id #uuid "697fcea0-b422-5fd9-8a3c-b4de3653327a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431482} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ec0f9e-bd19-5a45-8045-94296328023e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431494} {:answer/id #uuid "c75b25a4-08bf-5b34-b13b-1d43f8125742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431506} {:answer/id #uuid "c906702c-bb29-5e13-834a-3f940496c811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697fcea0-b422-5fd9-8a3c-b4de3653327a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431482} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab4196f-6b76-5bce-9655-3bb0e187a2a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d183a38-3bd7-5a3d-b0c0-6bbf656b9b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-130533" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\ny={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4.$ We differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2+Q=-4\\,⟹\\,Q=-2.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dabdd0c-2588-5f7d-8ccd-b68af2421ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7{e}^{-3x}-10{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342485} {:answer/id #uuid "49169b95-990f-59eb-8bb6-9cf98419ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x}-6{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342839} {:answer/id #uuid "a4feee5b-c504-529a-b361-ad69aa51b813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12{e}^{-3x}+9{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343692} {:answer/id #uuid "c6148395-0be0-5c18-b79a-ca9ffdf1bf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343975} {:answer/id #uuid "eb97d4ca-37d6-530d-8bb6-f0f612d82f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9{e}^{-3x}+6{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6148395-0be0-5c18-b79a-ca9ffdf1bf78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343975} :answer-field/id #uuid "18454ee3-e909-5372-948f-8f633a9d0c9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2c9bcd-a587-5660-97a8-aa4955c427cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b627c23d-f87b-5fd9-8e53-f2464eecff4d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Distinct Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26037b2c-fdab-548c-ba39-81bdf3515e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431467} {:answer/id #uuid "7569b8e5-0cd2-5f8b-acf3-e84018c38232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-5x+5}+2{e}^{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431486} {:answer/id #uuid "bc750cc4-f587-579a-b2ff-04773b6d8134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-5x+5}+3{e}^{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431501} {:answer/id #uuid "d405ca87-259a-5550-ad19-3cb0d9609b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{4x-4}-2{e}^{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431508} {:answer/id #uuid "ec87e771-a6a7-53b3-8bba-b2c753c87011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{e}^{-5x+5}+8{e}^{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7569b8e5-0cd2-5f8b-acf3-e84018c38232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-5x+5}+2{e}^{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431486} :answer-field/id #uuid "028dd5c8-1b67-5564-af86-02df34d419e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe7e11df-3bda-53fa-998a-37d8783a4f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-7789" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=7.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-5}+Be=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(1)=7$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-5}+Be=1 \\\\ -5A{e}^{-5}+Be=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-{e}^{5},B=2{e}^{-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{e}^{5}\\cdot {e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5x+5}+2{e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0137ee6e-c523-5606-ab61-bd96f6cd46e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431455} {:answer/id #uuid "1f869bf6-107f-5db7-b5a5-33d521155f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431464} {:answer/id #uuid "42d8c8b0-9be5-591b-b0ec-8b768f062a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431472} {:answer/id #uuid "462f6db9-3bd4-53ff-a35f-8656edd69646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431474} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd8cda8-05fa-59b2-9091-e867beec2408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-5x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f869bf6-107f-5db7-b5a5-33d521155f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431464} :answer-field/id #uuid "c771707c-db14-54e5-9719-2f5b8eb0740c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "385b9591-ad6c-5296-82bc-53313648dde5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7797" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=1.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A+B=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=1$ gives $B=5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+5x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeaa8919-6cd1-523d-89da-7ef2030fd077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} {:answer/id #uuid "31ad293a-a2de-5b04-a010-75b1e574916b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{e}^{4x-4}+9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393768} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7466a7-5e6b-57ce-b40f-1d86f392fa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{e}^{4x-4}-3x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393769} {:answer/id #uuid "2731b0a9-3ca5-56a5-b3d2-8efcf3d3f0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x-2}-6x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393770} {:answer/id #uuid "738f5cba-3a2c-5ec8-92f7-05586e62d819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x-2}-x{e}^{2x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaa8919-6cd1-523d-89da-7ef2030fd077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393767} :answer-field/id #uuid "656ffe0f-9fb3-55a1-98b5-b13db8a4f14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ccd8f3-5c55-5b1e-8569-1f7045c0f609" :question/math-academy-id "q-7798" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{4x}+4Bx{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4}+B{e}^{4}=2 \\\\ 4A{e}^{4}+5B{e}^{4}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=11{e}^{-4},B=-9{e}^{-4}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11{e}^{-4}\\cdot {e}^{4x}-9{e}^{-4}\\cdot x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =11{e}^{4x-4}-9x{e}^{4x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84e87d0d-806d-5587-849e-17d6999dbbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e8157a-552f-5c3e-9922-5f98c42236bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{x/2-1}+3x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345723} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa6a344-1c11-5d1d-91e2-7b17d052e60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345730} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb0744b-1328-5467-aff8-5eda9102546f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} {:answer/id #uuid "5e98c6cd-0b58-542c-859e-0b8cec34dba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{x/2-1}+2x{e}^{x/2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346167} {:answer/id #uuid "8abde012-2b17-5b1a-afc7-33d35b8535b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-3x/2+1}-2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb0744b-1328-5467-aff8-5eda9102546f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345893} :answer-field/id #uuid "db589b01-39df-5ab0-9143-384f9d7d07fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ece2165a-867c-5bfa-9095-1c580456d3cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7815" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\frac{9y}{4}=0,\\,y(1)={e}^{-1/2},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x/2}+Bx{e}^{-3x/2}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \nSubstituting $y(1)={e}^{-1/2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3/2}+B{e}^{-3/2} & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ A+B & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2x}+B{e}^{-3/2x}-\\frac{3}{2}Bx{e}^{-3/2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2}$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{-3/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}B & =\\frac{1}{2}e \\\\ -3A-B & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=e \\\\ -3A-B=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-e,B=2e.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-e\\cdot {e}^{-3x/2}+2e\\cdot x{e}^{-3x/2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x/2+1}+2x{e}^{-3x/2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9690a7-a7e2-5397-9fab-5932d5f57697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345480} {:answer/id #uuid "259386fa-930c-5ed2-8146-0410d65c7c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1db9ec-13d0-523f-beab-84f5738731bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345876} {:answer/id #uuid "d42af497-46f2-5618-b60b-d642f0f7e4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347206} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d750ad-e2fd-5a5b-a423-66b337485f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6288fe0-74a5-5333-b0d5-8d242026b44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9131a7ff-7e61-5a90-96bc-6b1cab75b7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7892" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-P{e}^{\\pi } & ={e}^{\\pi } \\\\ P & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=4{e}^{\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2P{e}^{\\pi }-2Q{e}^{\\pi } & =4{e}^{\\pi } \\\\ -2P-2Q & =4 \\\\ 2-2Q & =4 \\\\ Q & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-1$ and $Q=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b282211-1d23-5349-b3f7-ddda48199c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x-\\pi /6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431468} {:answer/id #uuid "50868c33-8407-589c-bfad-291f32fb6f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431477} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa32848-af4f-5ffd-ad94-45426b415ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x-\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431492} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0206d8-67a6-5880-99ad-0ae8bdd971eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb9cf92-1a16-58f2-9b54-96a4a29652b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50868c33-8407-589c-bfad-291f32fb6f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431477} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4eeea1c-8cea-5fa0-af41-255c4c40e45b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a78761c-d0ad-5651-b02a-e973e2385ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-7893" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+10{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)^{2}-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =1 \\\\ Q & ={e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-Q{e}^{-\\pi /6}-3P{e}^{-\\pi /6} & =2 \\\\ -Q-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ -{e}^{\\pi /6}-3P & =2{e}^{\\pi /6} \\\\ P & =-{e}^{\\pi /6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $Q={e}^{\\pi /6}$ and $P=-{e}^{\\pi /6}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+{e}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x+\\pi /6}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067e7b03-611d-5b4d-b180-2ffdccf4d076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431456} {:answer/id #uuid "59dfe9fd-988f-53c2-855f-b1614812537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431479} {:answer/id #uuid "7428c405-4302-51f7-bc5d-fea12e12217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431485} {:answer/id #uuid "aa31fab2-2bea-5ce6-a508-c29c038b4b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431495} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d3393f-9411-5f1f-a0aa-92424adb8c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7428c405-4302-51f7-bc5d-fea12e12217f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431485} :answer-field/id #uuid "408b0afd-e826-5064-a8e5-e44f09ba38cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e50b91c5-e53d-5ab3-9544-957263ee499e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81397" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),\n$$\n\nsolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2\\sqrt{3}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}=A\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,A=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152286 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ebb2332-3e40-5627-bc43-258ca4a80424" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e2251b-7d16-5372-af31-a605bcd8d61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355091} {:answer/id #uuid "35368f75-5659-5ae7-a5af-a8c1c96b7648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355141} {:answer/id #uuid "5b39f1ae-f133-5b7b-a1db-74e10797637e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)-4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355470} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec40822-5e09-542f-9d33-f9368a95d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355923} {:answer/id #uuid "a525c814-3a04-5b28-90af-15cdaca6df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a525c814-3a04-5b28-90af-15cdaca6df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79cbe3f-3a7d-5248-b7e4-045c51d53800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "169defb3-7918-5f63-aa50-008ef745a1e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94020" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2},$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{2}P\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+\\sqrt{2}Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=4\\sqrt{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}Q=4\\sqrt{2}\\,⟹\\,Q=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} {:answer/id #uuid "09bfb13a-5032-50df-8a5f-8f4a6c0a4bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354755} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5dcb2c-cea8-56b7-9456-0766f72b8509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355075} {:answer/id #uuid "49966142-bc9a-56bd-a8a1-fa56c7efffc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355318} {:answer/id #uuid "c19a7e5b-8d42-5925-8bf0-cfa378fb7bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7a07ed-713b-5880-92ea-6cbd8e8d8cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3243450-c950-543f-968a-f0bf49df75ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94025" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e5f532-d99f-5406-b006-f34aa9074819" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676537c8-4285-5aad-a757-a9d82bb77416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431481} {:answer/id #uuid "93f7ea21-c612-5811-b154-48179ccad499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431490} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4bbbd3-9f33-5dfb-b89b-72bf0b2ad247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431496} {:answer/id #uuid "bc331650-5409-5760-9fea-61f15da13246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431500} {:answer/id #uuid "d49931e9-d307-5540-8815-ad67d0cb20b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa4bbbd3-9f33-5dfb-b89b-72bf0b2ad247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431496} :answer-field/id #uuid "92b560c3-a3e3-5fae-81d1-ba12be4a1dcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad9f0a12-4237-5c82-9aa7-6fbbeb604773" :question/math-academy-id "q-94027" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{e}^{-2x}(-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-2P+3Q=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=1$ gives $Q=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
